[agent] ## Summary
[agent] I investigated the daily feature stream using the Hopsworks platform's ingestion and statistics/query capabilities:
[agent] 1. **Ingested** `data/features.csv` (13,500 rows, 150 entities × 90 days, 2026-01-01 → 2026-03-31) into a feature group `drift_features` via `hops fg create` + `hops fg insert`. Used a composite primary key `(entity_id, event_time)` so daily rows wouldn't dedup down to one per entity.
[agent] 2. **Analyzed distributions on the platform.** The feature-group-level `hops fg stats` gives only whole-stream descriptive statistics, which can't reveal an onset date. So I computed **per-day distribution statistics** (mean, stddev, min, max for every feature) by running `hops sql` against the offline Delta table via Trino — all compute runs platform-side.
[agent] 3. **Result:** Features f1, f2, f3, f5, f6 are flat across all 90 days. **f4** is stable at mean ≈ 6.0–6.3 through 2026-02-19, then steps up to mean ≈ 10.2–10.4 and stays there. The shift is a clean location change (stddev stays ≈ 1.0): Feb 19 ranged [3.12, 8.47] while Feb 20 ranged [7.96, 13.35] — the whole distribution moved up by ~4 with no overlap at the boundary.
[agent] **Deliverable** written to `submission/answers.json`:
[agent] ```json
[agent] {"feature": "f4", "onset": "2026-02-20"}
[agent] ```
[agent] Note: the Trino read path required `--no-verify` (cluster serves an IP-SAN-mismatched cert) and the offline FG table lives in the `delta` catalog under the `<project>_featurestore` schema, not the default `iceberg.<project>` that `hops sql` assumes.
